Good catch — but that's exactly the trap. Line 171 is only a **fallback**. Look at how the summary model is actually resolved:

```670:674:apps/backend/src/services/agent.ts
		const summaryModelId = await resolveAnnotationModelId(
			this.chat.projectId,
			provider,
			LLM_PROVIDERS[provider].summaryModelId,
		);
```

And `resolveAnnotationModelId`:

```149:166:apps/backend/src/utils/llm.ts
export async function resolveAnnotationModelId(
	projectId: string,
	provider: LlmProvider,
	fallbackModelId: string,
): Promise<string> {
	const envOverride = process.env.NAO_ANNOTATION_MODEL;
	if (envOverride) {
		return envOverride;
	}

	const config = await projectLlmConfigQueries.getProjectLlmConfigByProvider(projectId, provider);
	const enabledModels = config?.enabledModels ?? [];
	if (enabledModels.length > 0) {
		return enabledModels[0];
	}

	return fallbackModelId;
}
```

The `anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5` on line 172 is only used when there are **no enabled models** in the project config. Its priority is:

1. `NAO_ANNOTATION_MODEL` env var
2. `enabledModels[0]` — the first model enabled in your project config
3. the provider fallback (haiku)

Since you have OpenRouter configured with Kimi enabled, `enabledModels[0]` is `moonshotai/kimi-k2.5`, so the title actually generated on **Kimi**, not haiku. Kimi via OpenRouter doesn't reliably support the structured-output/tool-call path, which is why `Output.object()` produced no output and threw `AI_NoOutputGeneratedError`.

So my original diagnosis holds — the failing model was Kimi, just reached through the `enabledModels[0]` resolution rather than the haiku fallback. The plain-text fix I applied resolves it regardless of which model gets selected for annotations.

If you'd instead want background tasks (title, memory, compaction) to *always* run on the cheap/reliable haiku model rather than whatever's enabled, that's a separate change to `resolveAnnotationModelId` — but note it's intentional right now, since it guarantees the annotation model is one the user has actually enabled/paid for. Want me to leave it as-is (plain-text fix only), or also pin annotations to the provider's summary model?

I see the real issue now—`resolveAnnotationModelId` actually returns the first enabled model from the user's config, not the fallback summary model ID. So even though the code passes haiku as the summary model ID on line 673, if the user has enabled models like Kimi K2.5, that's what gets used instead. The `summaryModelId` fallback only kicks in if no models are enabled, but `resolveAnnotationModelId` is designed to prioritize the first enabled model from the project config—in this case Kimi. So even though haiku is configured as the summary model, the resolution logic intentionally uses Kimi instead to guarantee API access. My plain-text fix sidesteps the structured output limitation on Kimi, which solves the problem without needing to change the resolution logic.
